Here’s a quick guide on how to wire up a simple queue around `myWorkerFunction` with **five concurrent workers** and *no* explicit payload size restriction.

```js
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣ Define your worker logic.
//    Replace this stub with whatever async work you need to perform.
async function myWorkerFunction(task) {
  console.log(`Processing task ${task.id}`);

  /* Example asynchronous operation */
  await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, Math.random() * 1000));

  console.log(`Finished task ${task.id}`);
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣ Create the queue instance.
//
//   • `concurrency`: Number of parallel workers (here, 5).
//   • `payload`: Omitted → defaults to “process all queued items”.
//   • You’ll typically push raw data objects into the queue later.
//
const { Queue } = require('your‑preferred‑queue-lib'); // e.g., p-queue, bull, etc.

// Using BullMQ as an illustrative example:
const { Worker, Queue: BullQueue } = require('@bullmq/redis');
const connectionOptions = {};          // Redis config – adjust as needed

// Instantiate the underlying queue (storage layer)
const jobQueue = new BullQueue('tasks', { redis: connectionOptions });

// Attach our worker function to consume jobs
new Worker(
  'tasks',
  async job => {
    try {
      await myWorkerFunction(job.data);
    } catch (err) {
      console.error(`Error handling job #${job.id}:`, err);
      throw err;
    }
  },
  {
    connection: connectionOptions,
    concurrency: 5,           // <-- five workers running simultaneously
    // payload option isn’t exposed directly in BullMQ; 
    // each worker pulls one job at a time unless you batch manually.
  }
);

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣ Adding tasks to the queue:
//
//   Each item pushed becomes a job that will be picked up by one of the 5 workers.
function enqueueTask(data) {
  jobQueue.add('process-task', data); // `data` is passed straight through to myWorkerFunction
}

// Example usage:
enqueueTask({ id: 101, content: 'Hello' });
enqueueTask({ id: 102, content: 'World' });
// …add more tasks as required…

```

### What changed compared to a default setup?

| Feature | Default / Typical | Your configuration |
|---------|-------------------|--------------------|
| Concurrency | Often 1–10 depending on library | Explicitly set to **5** |
| Payload size | Many libraries pull one job per worker tick | By omitting `payload`, each worker simply grabs the next available job (i.e., unlimited). |

If you’re using another queue implementation (e.g., `p-queue`, `bee-queue`, `kue`, etc.), the core idea remains identical—just pass `{ concurrency: 5 }` to its constructor or options object while leaving out any payload/batch-size argument.

Happy queuing!
